```text
[CLICK SLIDE 4]
[POINT TO INFOGRAPHIC: LEFT SIDE]

This brings us to the core thesis of my research: we must transition from brittle, reactive rate-limiting rules to an adaptive, self-learning cognitive perimeter.

On the left side of this infographic, you see the traditional status quo. Requests stream in, an IP-based counter checks an isolated quota, and low-and-slow traffic slips past with zero resistance.

[POINT TO INFOGRAPHIC: RIGHT SIDE]

On the right, we introduce our proposed paradigm:
First, we stop relying on ephemeral IP addresses. Attackers lease and discard residential IPs for pennies; tracking IP reputation is a fool's errand.
Second, we capture immutable cryptographic invariants directly from the transport layer handshake.
Third, we feed streaming cross-IP feature distributions into a deep neural sequence model that learns the subtle temporal choreography of legitimate versus automated traffic.
Fourth, when distributed anomaly patterns emerge across time, our system dynamically calculates a bot probability score, triggering proportional, surgical throttling before user credentials are breached.

Instead of asking: «Has this specific IP sent too many packets?», our system asks: «Does the temporal trajectory of this global traffic stream match organic human behavior or automated coordinated orchestration?»
```

